  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table I column 'Fig. 8' (p. 899); Fig. 8 caption, text p. 901",
  "Notes": "Same Fabry-Perot tunnel layer filter as Fig. 6 but with slightly absorbing tunnel layers (1.38 - j0.01) to show the loss of peak transmittance\nSpecification: None (illustrates the effect of absorption k = 0.01 in the tunnel layers).\nReported: Text p. 901: for the p polarization the peak transmittance decreases to 49 % and the bandwidth increases to 400 A. Fig. 8: p peak ~0.49 near 0.48 um, s peak ~0.73 near 0.58 um (plot).\nNotes: Identical to Baumeister1967-Fig6-FP-tunnel except the tunnel layers are 1.38 - j0.01 (k = 0.01, positive = absorbing). Same assumptions (layer 2 = one quarter wave at 1.837 um; 60 deg in the 1.72 medium, angle not printed). Own TMM check: p peak T = 0.489 at 0.478 um, s peak T = 0.727 at 0.577 um - matches the quoted 49 %, confirming the 60 deg / quarter-wave-at-1.837-um reading.\nLayers from Table I column 'Fig. 8' (p. 899); Fig. 8 caption, text p. 901; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours p peak T 48.9 %; paper p peak 49 % (p. 901).",
